Track 2: Did the Tape Hold Water?
- Story & Context: Camped under patched military canvas outside the dismantled relay station at Jabal Kharaz, the merchant Kaddour spent three days picking melted solder out of a grey FM workstation he had bartered for two barrels of diesel. His throat was raw from inhaling burnt flux and kerosene fumes as he admitted to his caravan mates that he had warped the tracking head by washing dust from the circuit board with lukewarm canteen water during a sandstorm. To disguise the shuddering tape drag, he tuned his lever harp to the machine's erratic 138-BPM clock and muttered a dry, low-register confession through an ungrounded dynamic microphone: "spill the grain before you count the teeth."
